  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday through next Friday)\nIssued at 1226 PM CDT Sat Sep 26 2026\n\nConcern is increasing for a significant multi day rain event over\na large area and including all of our CWA. Previous rainfall the \nlast 2 days has saturated the soil and so concern is high that if \nheavy rain occurs (which is looking more likely) then signficant \nflooding/flash flooding is a distinct possibility. Tropical \nmoisture from the remnants of Hurricanes Odalys and Polo will get \ncaught up in the flow of a developing trough digging into the \nsouthwestern states early next week. The combination of this \nunseasonable surge of tropical moisture along with the approaching\nstrong trough in the southwestern states ...and given the already\nsaturated ground from heavy rains over the past couple of days...\nsuggests a strong possibility of significant flooding and flash \nflooding. The latest NIMNAT call with WPC is showing they will be \nputting a Day 3 and Day 4 Moderate Risk for excessive \nrain....first over the southwestern states and then over a large \narea from southeast AZ into most of the Panhandles region. Our \narea has the most saturated ground of any of these areas due to \nthe recent heavy rains....so that will become a problem quickly. \nAnother concern, especially Tuesday evening will be the \npossibility of some isolated severe weather. The NAM sounding for \nTuesday evening just north of Amarillo is showing a very moist \nprofile but with about 1800 j/kg of CAPE. Even more interesting is\nthe hodograph showing a nice curved area with ~ 50kt of 0-6km \nshear and a SRH of around 310. This environment looks favorable \nfor mini supercells and will need to monitored closely. A couple \nof days ago in a similar tropical environment... with just a \nlittle better shear in the north, there was a funnel cloud \nobserved in the northern panhandle. This situation promises much \nbetter shear than then and a better environment. Due to the event \nstill being 4 days out this is not etched in stone. However, with \nthe models trending in the same direction and having done so for \nseveral days now, confidence is increasing in a potential higher \nend event for our area. If these trends continue thru the weekeend\nthen a Flood or Flash Flood Watch will likely be needed by the \nbeginning of the week.",
